<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/za-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Za dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Za dynasty was an early ruling dynasty of the Songhai people, centred on Gao in the bend of the Niger River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zababa"><span class="enc-name">Zababa</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Zababa was a Mesopotamian god of war and the patron deity of the city of Kish.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zabdas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zabdas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zabdas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zabdas was the leading general of Zenobia of Palmyra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zabibe"><span class="enc-name">Zabibe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zabibe was a queen of the Arabs, probably of Qedar, in the 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zabid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zabid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zabid</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zabid is a town in the Tihama coastal plain of western Yemen, founded around 820 by Muhammad ibn Ziyad as the capital of the Ziyadid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zabljak-crnojevica"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zabljak-crnojevica.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Žabljak Crnojevića</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Žabljak Crnojevića is a ruined fortified town on a rocky hill near the north-western shore of Lake Skadar in Montenegro.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zabt-system"><span class="enc-name">Zabt system</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The zabt system was a method of land revenue assessment in the Mughal Empire, developed under Akbar and associated with his minister Todar Mal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zabulistan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zabulistan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zabulistan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zabulistan was a historical region of south-eastern Afghanistan around Ghazni and the upper valleys of the Helmand and Arghandab.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zacatecas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zacatecas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zacatecas is a city in north-central Mexico founded after the discovery of rich silver deposits in 1546.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zaccaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zaccaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zaccaria</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zaccaria were a Genoese merchant family who built an Aegean domain in the late 13th and 14th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zacharias-ursinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zacharias-ursinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zacharias Ursinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zacharias Ursinus was a German Reformed theologian, born in Breslau and a pupil of Philip Melanchthon at Wittenberg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zacpeten"><span class="enc-name">Zacpetén</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zacpetén is a Maya archaeological site on a peninsula in Lake Salpetén in the Petén department of northern Guatemala.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zadar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zadar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zadar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zadar is a city on the Adriatic coast of Croatia, known in Italian as Zara.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zadok"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zadok.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zadok</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zadok was a priest of King David and Solomon according to the Hebrew Bible.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zafadola"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zafadola.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zafadola</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zafadola, as Christians called Ahmad Sayf al-Dawla, was the last prince of the Hudid dynasty of Zaragoza.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zafar-yemen"><span class="enc-name">Zafar (Yemen)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zafar was the capital of the Himyarite Kingdom in the highlands of south-western Arabia, south of Sanaa in modern Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zafarnama"><span class="enc-name">Zafarnama</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zafarnama, or Book of Victory, is a Persian chronicle of the life and conquests of Timur, written under Timurid patronage.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/za